(def ^:private cuda-tested-class-fragments
  "The name fragments of the tested device class's KEY -- row (iv) of
  the operator-aware row's created rows (ADR-0361's 2026-10-25 seventh
  amendment): the NARROWER device-class key (eleventh amendment). The
  tested device is the ONE device where the NVIDIA CUDA route's
  measurements ran: the fleet's CUDA host NVIDIA Jetson AGX Xavier
  (L4T R35.4.1, sm_72). The key is BOTH fragments \"xavier\" and
  \"agx\" sitting in ONE name part (case-insensitively, the uB5G
  per-name-part idiom of kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets --
  evidence about a NAMED device acts on that device's name and never
  past it) AND the reported compute capability 7.2
  (cuda-measured-compute-capability) -- fail closed at every key. This
  REPLACES the single fragment \"xavier\": under it a Jetson
  Xavier NX descriptor (also sm_72, name carries \"xavier\" but
  not \"agx\") read as the tested class and would have received
  every entry-contract record -- pooled AND per-op (row (iv) names
  both layers' records) -- with NO measurement behind it. The
  fragments must sit in ONE name part: the three name keys are
  alternate spellings of the device's name, not a fragment kit to be
  assembled across keys. NAMED RESIDUAL, NOT HIDDEN: the key is still
  name-based (the descriptor surface carries :device/:name/:vendor
  strings and :compute-capability -- no device-id, UUID or PCI-id
  axis exists and none is invented here), so any name part carrying
  both fragments with a reported 7.2 still reads as the tested class:
  \"Jetson AGX Xavier Industrial\" (also sm_72) is the concrete
  residual. A device-id key needs a producer fact that does not exist
  (PROPOSED, its own row if a producer ever supplies one). NOT
  NARROWED HERE: kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets' workgroup rows
  keep their own coarser uB5G key (:measured-device \"xavier\") --
  that table's scoping is its own decision and untouched by this row."
  ["xavier" "agx"])

(def ^:private cuda-measured-compute-capability
  "The reported compute capability of the tested device class: 7.2
  (sm_72 -- the -arch the corpora compiled with and the capability the
  two adopted sessions ran on). The measurement contract keys the entry
  to the tested device's name AND its reported compute capability: a
  descriptor reporting anything else (7.0, 7.5, a string, nothing) is
  not the tested device class and no record attaches -- fail closed,
  exactly like the Apple contract's subgroup-size key."
  7.2)

(defn- cuda-tested-class?
  "Whether DEV is the tested device class (cuda-tested-class-fragments
  at its letter): ONE name part -- :device, :name, or the :vendor name
  key, the same three keys uB5G reads in
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets/recommend -- carries BOTH name
  fragments case-insensitively, AND the descriptor reports the compute
  capability 7.2 exactly (a string, another number, or nothing is not
  the class). Both keys, fail closed; an unnamed (vendor-id-only)
  descriptor answers false (no name, no evidence to act on). This one
  predicate keys BOTH entry-contract layers -- cuda-entry-cell
  (pooled) and cuda-entry-op (per-op) -- so the narrowed key names the
  per-op records too (row (iv) at its letter). The tested device's
  own reported name carries both fragments (\"NVIDIA Jetson AGX
  Xavier\"); a Jetson Xavier NX (\"Jetson Xavier NX\", also 7.2)
  fails the fragment key and attaches nothing."
  [dev]
  (and (boolean (some (fn [part]
                        (and (string? part)
                             (not (str/blank? part))
                             (every? (fn [fragment]
                                       (str/includes? (str/lower part) fragment))
                                     cuda-tested-class-fragments)))
                      [(:device dev) (:name dev) (:vendor dev)]))
       (= cuda-measured-compute-capability (:compute-capability dev))))

(def ^:private cuda-measured-channel
  "The channel the contract-satisfying measurements ran through: the
  CUDA paired-timing instrument (scripts/gpu-cuda-reduce-timing.cu, the
  ADR-0358 paired-statistic instrument class) dispatching the emitters'
  own CUDA bytes natively on the tested host under nvcc 11.4 -- one
  fresh process per pair block, PAIRS=20 interleaved, WARMUP=2 per arm,
  AB/BA alternating, gpu_ns CUDA-event + host_ns CLOCK_MONOTONIC,
  untimed verified read-back per arm."
  :native-cuda)

(def cuda-cell-entries
  "The CUDA route's entered cell groups under the entry contract
  (ADR-0361's 2026-10-25 fifth amendment): entry is per (W, shape)
  GROUP of cells across pair types, arm v entering iff for every other
  arm u the (v, u) pair cell is REGIME-INVARIANT with side \"v
  faster\" in BOTH clocks (rule 2 at the cell's letter, unique-winner +
  both-clocks-or-refuse); anything else REFUSES and keeps the design
  ladder's answer with no record. This table is the ENTRY CENSUS over
  the two committed adopted sessions
  (docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-cell-census-run{1,2}-20261025/census.txt,
  re-derived byte-identically from the committed measure-logs): winner
  = :warp-shuffle-reduce (the design answer) at 10 of 12 (W, shape)
  groups -- W = 32/128/256/512/1024 x n = 1000003/1048576 -- both
  metrics, identically in both sessions; REFUSE at W = 64 both shapes
  (the warp-shuffle/subgroup cell straddles in both metrics and both
  sessions -- no winner exists); ZERO flips. Each map value is ONE
  entered (W, shape) group per listed shape: :winner is the group's
  unique winner (a winner equal to the design answer CONFIRMS -- answer
  text unchanged, :measured? true with the record; a differing winner
  FLIPS the cell -- no entry here carries one, so nothing flips), and
  :shapes is QUALIFYING shapes only (a shape whose group refused is not
  listed and extends no record -- W = 64 has no entry at all).
  :evidence is the contract's own record kinds: the group's own
  session's runs (run 1, the adopted session each group's entry was
  derived from) plus sanctioned cross-day replications (none exist yet
  -- run 2 is a SAME-DAY second session and is flip-note PROSE ONLY,
  ADR-0373's naming rule by citation: it corroborates the 12/12
  cross-session identity and names no record kind here). The confirm
  note lives in this docstring, the ADR-0361 amendment and the evidence
  record (docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-cell-entry-wiring-20261025.txt) -- the
  entry contract freezes answer text on confirm, so NO note is appended
  to :basis anywhere; variant-bases' design-assumption prose is design
  rationale and is not rewritten by a confirm. Scope (contract point 5):
  select-variant answer cells of the CUDA route ONLY, sign claims stay
  CHANNEL-SCOPED and DEVICE-SCOPED, no workgroup row answers or moves,
  and rung-2 answer cells (:subgroup-reduce where the warp gate does not
  reach -- a descriptor reporting NO compute capability) can never
  attach a record: the tested class reports sm_72 by definition."
  {32 {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
       :shapes [1000003 1048576]
       :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-claimprotocol-run1-20261025"]}
   128 {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
        :shapes [1000003 1048576]
        :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-claimprotocol-run1-20261025"]}
   256 {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
        :shapes [1000003 1048576]
        :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-claimprotocol-run1-20261025"]}
   512 {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
        :shapes [1000003 1048576]
        :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-claimprotocol-run1-20261025"]}
   1024 {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
         :shapes [1000003 1048576]
         :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-claimprotocol-run1-20261025"]}})

(def ^:private cuda-measured-device-class
  "The tested device class as the entry contract's record shape (3)
  spells it in a record's :device: \"xavier sm_72\" -- the class name
  and the reported compute capability named together, so the record
  names the class and no claim extends past either key. The label is
  UNCHANGED by the narrower device-class key (row (iv), the eleventh
  amendment): record shape (3) stays byte-identical -- what narrowed
  is the KEY deciding which descriptors ARE this class
  (cuda-tested-class?: both name fragments in one name part AND the
  reported 7.2), not the record's :device text. NAMED RESIDUAL, NOT
  HIDDEN: the key is still name-based and a name part carrying both
  fragments with a reported 7.2 still reads as the class --
  \"Jetson AGX Xavier Industrial\" (also sm_72) is the concrete
  residual; a device-id key needs a producer fact that does not
  exist (PROPOSED, its own row if a producer ever supplies one). NOT
  NARROWED HERE: kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets' workgroup rows
  keep their own coarser uB5G key (:measured-device \"xavier\") --
  that table's scoping is untouched by this row."
  "xavier sm_72")

(def ^:private cuda-flip-note
  "The note a FLIPPED CUDA-route cell carries in its :basis -- entry
  contract point 4's flip arm. No entry in cuda-cell-entries carries a
  differing winner today (ZERO flips in the entry census), so no answer
  carries this note yet; it exists so the flip arm is implemented, not
  invented at first need. It names the flip's ground (the cell group's
  unique winner under the entry contract) and its scope (xavier sm_72,
  the CUDA route, the record's shapes and operators -- nothing wider)."
  "MEASURED FLIP (entry contract, ADR-0361 fifth amendment): the
  committed cell census's unique winner at this (W, shape) group on the
  tested device class (xavier sm_72, CUDA paired-timing channel) is the
  variant this cell answers -- REGIME-INVARIANT faster than every rival
  in BOTH clocks (rule 2 at the cell unit's letter) -- over the measured
  shapes and operators named in the :measured-cell record this answer
  carries. DEVICE-SCOPED and CHANNEL-SCOPED: xavier sm_72 on the CUDA
  route only; no workgroup row answers or moves (ADR-0355).")

(defn- cuda-entry-cell
  "The entry contract (ADR-0361's 2026-10-25 fifth amendment) applied to
  ONE CUDA-route answer cell of the design ladder. Answers the group's
  entry as {:outcome :confirm|:flip :variant winner :record {...}} when
  a cuda-cell-entries group keys this cell, and nil otherwise -- REFUSE
  at its letter: the design ladder's answer stands with no record. The
  keys are the tested device class (cuda-tested-class?: BOTH name
  fragments \"xavier\" + \"agx\" in ONE name part AND the reported
  compute capability 7.2 -- fail closed at every key; the narrower
  device-class key, row (iv)), the measured
  channel's route (:cuda-v1 under either spelling; a CUDA measurement
  never acts on an MSL/WGSL/GLSL cell -- contract point 5), the reduce
  shape selected as a whole (one of :sum/:max/:min, no per-operator
  entry), and a workgroup width whose group ENTERED (W = 64's groups
  refused -- the straddle has no winner -- and the valid rung-1 width
  band is exactly 32/64/128/256/512/1024, so 64 is the band's only
  non-entry). Rung-2 answer cells (:subgroup-reduce where the
  warp gate does not reach -- a descriptor reporting NO compute
  capability) can never attach a record: the tested class reports 7.2
  by definition. :confirm means the group's unique winner EQUALS the
  design answer -- answer text is frozen (contract point 4: no note is
  appended to :basis anywhere), :measured? goes true with the record;
  :flip means a differing winner -- the cell answers the winner with the
  record and cuda-flip-note (no entry here carries one today). LAYERING
  (the seventh amendment's precedence rule): this pooled arm answers
  everywhere NO per-op record applies -- cuda-entry-op answers its
  operator at its W FIRST (its record keys :operators #{op}), and this
  arm's record (:operators #{:sum :max :min} whole) stands wherever the
  per-op table has no entry."
  [dev route kir design-variant]
  (when (and (= :cuda-v1 route)
             (= :warp-shuffle-reduce design-variant)
             (cuda-tested-class? dev)
             (= :reduce (:kernel/op kir))
             (contains? #{:sum :max :min} (:kernel/operator kir)))
    (when-let [entry (get cuda-cell-entries (:kernel/workgroup-size kir))]
      (let [winner (:winner entry)]
        {:outcome (if (= winner design-variant) :confirm :flip)
         :variant winner
         :flip-note cuda-flip-note
         :record {:device cuda-measured-device-class
                  :channel cuda-measured-channel
                  :route :cuda-v1
                  :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
                  :operators #{:sum :max :min}
                  :shapes (:shapes entry)
                  :evidence (:evidence entry)}}))))

(def cuda-op-entries
  "The CUDA route's PER-OPERATOR entry table under the operator-aware
  entry contract (ADR-0361's 2026-10-25 seventh amendment, this row =
  its row (i) wiring). The entry group is (op, W) -- one reduce
  operator at one workgroup width -- with :shapes QUALIFYING shapes
  only and a SINGLE-OP record (:operators #{op}, contract point 2:
  the pooled record shape (3) with single-op :operators). The entry
  rule (contract point 3) is unique-winner + both-clocks + cross-run
  invariance: a group enters iff its unique winner (faster than BOTH
  rivals -- the three-arm ladder) holds one-sided in EVERY qualifying
  reading (every admissible session x both clocks x every qualifying
  shape); anything else REFUSES and keeps the standing answer with no
  record. The replication requirement (contract point 4) is the
  pre-registration's teeth: EVERY per-op entry, confirm or flip,
  requires at least TWO fresh paired-timing sessions run UNDER the
  seventh amendment's registration on at least TWO different UTC days
  agreeing in every reading of both. MATERIALIZATION (rejection (e) at
  its letter): the per-op layer only ADDS where the pooled layer
  refused or an admissible per-op winner differs -- at the 15 (op, W)
  groups whose winner equals the standing pooled answer NO per-op
  record materializes (the pooled record stands), so the census
  populates exactly three groups. THE TABLE HAS ENTERED (the
  2026-10-26 cross-day agreement adjudication, ADR-0361's thirteenth
  amendment -- sessions A + B, 2026-10-25 and 2026-10-26 UTC, both
  fresh under the registration, agreeing in every reading of both; the
  projection's three groups, now a MEASUREMENT-BACKED verdict under
  the registration, no longer a projection): (:sum, 64) FLIP (the
  winner :subgroup-reduce differs from the design rung-1 answer -- the
  cell answers the winner with this table's record and
  cuda-op-flip-note) and (:max, 64) and (:min, 64) CONFIRM (the winner
  equals the design answer -- answer text frozen, :measured? true with
  the record -- the pooled layer refused at W = 64, so the per-op
  layer adds); both shapes (1000003/1048576) qualify at every entered
  group and the 15 (op, W) groups at W = 32/128/256/512/1024 x
  {sum, max, min} materialize NO record (winner = standing pooled
  answer -- rejection (e) at its letter). Contract point 5 scope
  unchanged: CUDA route only, sign claims CHANNEL-SCOPED and
  DEVICE-SCOPED to the tested class (xavier sm_72), no workgroup row
  answers or moves. Each map value:
  :winner the group's unique winner (equal to the standing answer
  CONFIRMS -- answer text frozen; differing FLIPS -- the cell answers
  the winner with the record and cuda-op-flip-note), :shapes
  qualifying-only, :evidence the entry's own record kinds (the
  qualifying sessions' runs -- a same-day second session is flip-note
  PROSE ONLY, ADR-0373's naming rule by citation)."
  {[:sum 64] {:winner :subgroup-reduce
              :shapes [1000003 1048576]
              :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-registered-sessionA-20261025"
                         "docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-registered-sessionB-20261026"]}
   [:max 64] {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
              :shapes [1000003 1048576]
              :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-registered-sessionA-20261025"
                         "docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-registered-sessionB-20261026"]}
   [:min 64] {:winner :warp-shuffle-reduce
              :shapes [1000003 1048576]
              :evidence ["docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-registered-sessionA-20261025"
                         "docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-registered-sessionB-20261026"]}})

(def ^:private cuda-op-flip-note
  "The note a FLIPPED per-op cell carries in its :basis -- the
  operator-aware entry contract's flip arm (seventh amendment). ONE
  real per-op entry carries a differing winner today -- (:sum, 64)
  answers it, the FIRST real flip on the CUDA route, landed from the
  2026-10-26 cross-day adjudication -- so the :sum cells at W = 64 on
  the tested class carry this note. It names the flip's ground (the (op, W) group's unique winner
  under the per-op entry rule) and its scope: THIS operator only, the
  measured shapes named in the :measured-cell record this answer
  carries, DEVICE-SCOPED and CHANNEL-SCOPED (xavier sm_72 on the CUDA
  route only), and no workgroup row answers or moves (ADR-0355)."
  "MEASURED FLIP (per-op entry contract, ADR-0361 seventh amendment):
  the committed per-op census's unique winner at this (op, W) group on
  the tested device class (xavier sm_72, CUDA paired-timing channel) is
  the variant this cell answers -- unique-winner + both-clocks +
  cross-run invariance (every qualifying reading one-sided) -- for THIS
  operator only, over the measured shapes named in the :measured-cell
  record this answer carries. DEVICE-SCOPED and CHANNEL-SCOPED: xavier
  sm_72 on the CUDA route only; no workgroup row answers or moves
  (ADR-0355).")

(defn- cuda-entry-op
  "The operator-aware entry contract (ADR-0361's 2026-10-25 seventh
  amendment, row (i)) applied to ONE CUDA-route answer cell: the per-op
  arm of the entry application, answering the (op, W) group's entry as
  {:outcome :confirm|:flip :variant winner :flip-note
  cuda-op-flip-note :record {...}} when cuda-op-entries keys this
  cell's operator and width, and nil otherwise -- REFUSE at its
  letter. The keys are exactly the pooled arm's keys (the tested
  device class -- cuda-tested-class?: BOTH name fragments \"xavier\" +
  \"agx\" in ONE name part AND the reported compute capability 7.2,
  fail closed at every key; the narrower device-class key, row (iv),
  which names the per-op records too; the measured channel's route
  :cuda-v1 under either spelling; the reduce op with an operator in
  #{:sum :max :min}; a design-ladder rung-1 answer) plus the entry's
  OWN key: [:kernel/operator :kernel/workgroup-size] must be in
  cuda-op-entries. PRECEDENCE (contract point 1): where this arm
  answers, its record (:operators #{op}) answers its operator at its
  W OVER the pooled record -- select-variant consults this arm first
  and the pooled arm answers everywhere no per-op record applies.
  Rung-2 answer cells can never attach a record (the tested class
  reports 7.2 by definition); a non-reduce kernel or an unknown
  operator keys nothing. The record shape is the pooled record's (3)
  with single-op :operators: :device cuda-measured-device-class,
  :channel cuda-measured-channel, :route :cuda-v1, :workgroup-size,
  :operators #{op}, :shapes qualifying-only, :evidence the entry's."
  [dev route kir design-variant]
  (when (and (= :cuda-v1 route)
             (= :warp-shuffle-reduce design-variant)
             (cuda-tested-class? dev)
             (= :reduce (:kernel/op kir))
             (contains? #{:sum :max :min} (:kernel/operator kir)))
    (when-let [entry (get cuda-op-entries [(:kernel/operator kir)
                                           (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)])]
      (let [winner (:winner entry)]
        {:outcome (if (= winner design-variant) :confirm :flip)
         :variant winner
         :flip-note cuda-op-flip-note
         :record {:device cuda-measured-device-class
                  :channel cuda-measured-channel
                  :route :cuda-v1
                  :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
                  :operators #{(:kernel/operator kir)}
                  :shapes (:shapes entry)
                  :evidence (:evidence entry)}}))))

(def variant-bases
  "Why each variant exists. Design rationale, not measurement evidence."
  {:baseline
   "ADR-0002 v1 tree reduction over workgroup shared memory; the portable
    fallback. Execution record (reconciled 2026-10-23): NO variant has an
    execution path in this repository today -- the wire-42 route died with
    the C loader and ADR 0371's provider is the replacement track -- and
    :subgroup-reduce has EXECUTED on Apple M1 Max through measurement
    harnesses (GLSL arm via wire-42/MoltenVK, PR #1347/#1348, 2026-10-19;
    MSL arm via a native Metal harness, ADR-0360's measurement amendment,
    2026-10-23), losing to :baseline in both arms, and
    :simdgroup-reduce has EXECUTED on the same device through the
    native-Metal harness (2026-10-23/24, W = 32/64/128/256/512/1024),
    BEATING
    :baseline at all six cells (paired median ratios
    0.657/0.658/0.650 at W = 32,
    0.719/0.728/0.729 at W = 64,
    0.775/0.789/0.789 at W = 128,
    0.790/0.800/0.799 at W = 256, 0.815/0.823/0.824 at W = 512 and
    0.840/0.847/0.850 at W = 1024 for :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol
    rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18 per-run ratios below 1.0 at
    each)."
   :warp-shuffle-reduce
   "NVIDIA (sm_70+) warp-shuffle reduce: a per-warp __shfl_down_sync fold,
    then one cross-warp stage through shared memory -- one barrier instead
    of the baseline's log2(W) cascade. The design assumption (UNMEASURED) is
    that removing the barrier cascade wins on NVIDIA hardware. It replaces no
    workgroup row and changes no reduction semantics: the same elements are
    grouped in a different order (f32 :sum may differ in the low bits;
    :max/:min are order-independent)."
   :subgroup-reduce
   "Intel (JUN-59, ADR-0356), ARM (JUN-60, ADR-0357), Apple (JUN-58,
    ADR-0360 -- the Vulkan route, the MSL route, and the WGSL route
    since the 2026-10-24 Apple WGSL-route decision,
    docs/evidence/gpu/apple-wgsl-route-decision-20261024.txt), NVIDIA (JUN-57, ADR-0361 -- the Vulkan route, and the
    CUDA route as the ladder's second rung under :warp-shuffle-reduce's
    precedence; the WGSL route too since the 2026-10-24 NVIDIA WGSL-route
    decision, docs/evidence/gpu/nvidia-wgsl-route-decision-20261024.txt)
    and AMD (JUN-84, ADR-0364 -- the Vulkan and WGSL routes)
    subgroup reduce: an
    intra-subgroup fold whose width is queried at RUNTIME (gl_SubgroupSize)
    and never baked in -- Intel subgroup size is device-chosen among
    8/16/32, Mali's spans 4..32, Apple's SIMD-group is 32 and RDNA's wave
    is 32 or 64 as the shader chooses, so a baked
    width would be wrong somewhere. The
    fold needs no barriers and no shared memory; one write barrier plus a
    cross-subgroup tree over the W/S partials replaces the baseline
    log2(W)-stage cascade (4/3/2 stages at W = 128 for S = 8/16/32 against
    the baseline's 7 -- stage arithmetic, not a speed claim). The design
    assumption (UNMEASURED) is that removing most of the barrier cascade
    wins on Intel, Mali, Apple and NVIDIA hardware. The assumption is
    UNMEASURED on Intel, Mali and NVIDIA (no hardware there has run the
    variant) and MEASURED-CONTRADICTED on Apple: Apple M1 Max ran the
    variant twice and it lost to :baseline both times (GLSL arm via
    wire-42/MoltenVK, PR #1347/#1348, 2026-10-19: ~20-27% slower at W=256
    and never faster over W=32..256; MSL arm via a native Metal harness,
    ADR-0360's measurement amendment, 2026-10-23: ~22% slower at W=256
    across :sum/:max/:min, and the same day's native-Metal W-sweep: still
    slower at W=512 and W=1024, paired median ratios 1.19-1.20 and
    1.18-1.19) -- one device and narrow shape coverage. The
    measured cell DECIDED the row's fate on 2026-10-23 (ADR-0360's second
    decision): the row is NARROWED to the measured device -- a descriptor
    naming the measured device answers :baseline, because that device
    measured the variant slower -- and stands (UNMEASURED) on every other
    device. It
    replaces no workgroup row. Semantics, scoped as ADR-0357 P1 requires:
    the reduction groups the same admitted elements as the :baseline tree
    in a different order -- f32 :sum agrees with the baseline to low bits on
    well-conditioned inputs, and on cancellation-sensitive inputs the
    divergence is NOT confined to the low bits and stays an UNMEASURED
    divergence regime from the baseline; :max/:min are order-independent
    within the ordinary-value domain only (no NaN payloads, no negative
    zeros -- ADR-0356 rule 2)."
   :simdgroup-reduce
   "Apple (JUN-58, ADR-0373) Metal-native SIMD-group reduce: the reduce
    shape on the MSL route ONLY, whose intra-SIMD-group fold is ONE Metal
    SIMD-group reduction intrinsic per operator (simd_sum / simd_max /
    simd_min) instead of the shuffle ladder -- everything above it is the
    shared fold, byte-for-byte :subgroup-reduce's MSL arm (the partials
    array, the one write barrier, the cross-SIMD-group tree). It is a
    SEPARATE variant, never a rewrite of :subgroup-reduce. Selection is
    the MSL ladder (rule 3 step 2): the vendor-native FIRST rung for an
    Apple MSL descriptor reporting the fold's own rule-6 fact
    (:simd-reduce -- the device's SIMD-group exposes the reduction
    intrinsics) and a reported SIMD-group width dividing W;
    :basic/:shuffle-relative are the SHUFFLE fold's facts and are not
    required here (the tree uses threadgroup barriers). The measured-cell
    carve-out outranks it everywhere but this row's OWN flip cells: at
    W = 32, W = 64, W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on the
    measured
    class (Apple
    M1 Max,
    reported subgroup size
    32, MSL route, n = 1000003) the native-Metal measurements of
    2026-10-23/24 measured this row FASTER than :baseline (paired median
    ratios 0.657/0.658/0.650 at W = 32,
    0.719/0.728/0.729 at W = 64,
    0.775/0.789/0.789 at W = 128,
    0.790/0.800/0.799 at W = 256, 0.815/0.823/0.824 at W = 512
    and 0.840/0.847/0.850 at W = 1024
    for :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol rule 2
    REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER at all six cells) and the flip contract
    answers it there,
    MEASURED (apple-simdgroup-flip-note); those six are every width the
    row admits, so the class's W band is flipped end to end and the
    carve-out's :baseline survives on the class only where the flip's
    keys do not hold (e.g. a mismatched reported SIMD-group width).
    Every other cell stays closed to design rows. The
    design assumption (UNMEASURED) is that one reduction intrinsic beats
    the shuffle ladder anywhere else -- six cells, one shape
    (n = 1000003) and one device are measured; the shuffle fold's own rationale
    measured CONTRADICTED on that device (M1 Max); that refutation
    neither condemns nor blesses this row outside the flip cells. No
    speed claim outside the flip cells' own recorded scope. Semantics:
    :sum is scoped to low bits (the intrinsic's association order is the
    vendor's own, ADR-0357 P1 option b); :max/:min are bit-for-bit within
    the ordinary-value domain only (non-NaN payloads, no negative zeros).
    It replaces no workgroup row."})

(defn select-variant
  "The kernel variant for a (vendor, kir) pair, optionally scoped to the
  emission `target` and the `device` descriptor the dispatch layers carry.
  TOTAL and deterministic: the same arguments answer the same map, forever.

  The rows (ADR-0356 as amended by ADR-0357; every answer carries
  :measured? false except the measured cells of the Apple row's narrowing
  on the MSL route -- ADR-0360 question (1)'s decision, tested in
  the-measured-cells-flip-the-honesty-lock -- and the CUDA route's
  entry-contract cells on the tested device class (xavier sm_72, W =
  32/128/256/512/1024, ADR-0361's sixth amendment, tested in
  the-cuda-cell-entry-wiring-confirms-the-design-answer)):

  - :warp-shuffle-reduce -- NVIDIA reduction kernels over :sum/:max/:min
    whose workgroup reaches one full warp, on the CUDA route (:cuda or
    :cuda-v1), on a device confirming compute capability 7.0+ (rule 6: the
    sm_70+ floor in variant-bases is a real requirement; a descriptor that
    does not confirm it answers :baseline, fail closed).

  - :subgroup-reduce -- Intel (JUN-59), ARM (JUN-60), Apple (JUN-58,
    ADR-0360), NVIDIA (JUN-57, ADR-0361) and AMD
    (JUN-84, ADR-0364) reduction
    kernels
    over :sum/:max/:min with a workgroup of at least one subgroup floor
    (the emission's own predicate, kotoba.compiler.accelerator-subgroup-
    reduce/applicable?), on the Vulkan route (:vulkan or :glsl-v1) for all
    five vendors, for Intel, ARM, AMD, NVIDIA and Apple (ADR-0363; for :amd
    ADR-0364; for :nvidia the 2026-10-24 NVIDIA WGSL-route decision,
    JUN-57's own decision,
    docs/evidence/gpu/nvidia-wgsl-route-decision-20261024.txt; for :apple
    the 2026-10-24 Apple WGSL-route decision, JUN-58's own decision,
    docs/evidence/gpu/apple-wgsl-route-decision-20261024.txt)
    on the WGSL route
    (:webgpu or :wgsl-v1 -- the vendor-default target for Intel, ARM and
    AMD; NVIDIA and Apple reach the route by naming it, their
    vendor-default targets are the CUDA and MSL routes), for
    Apple ONLY (ADR-0360's 2026-10-23 amendment, JUN-58's own decision) on
    the MSL route (:metal or :msl-v1 -- Apple's vendor-default target),
    and for NVIDIA (ADR-0361's same-day amendment, JUN-57's own decision)
    on the CUDA route (:cuda or :cuda-v1) as the SECOND rung of that
    route's ladder: :warp-shuffle-reduce is checked first and keeps
    precedence whenever its gate holds (a CONFIRMED compute capability
    7.0+ and W >= 32) -- every answer it gives is unchanged -- and this
    row answers only where the warp row's gate does not reach: a
    descriptor that reports the rule-6 subgroup facts and NO compute
    capability gets the parametric fold instead of :baseline (the same
    treatment the Vulkan row already gives an absent capability). The
    ladder is total and deterministic: :warp-shuffle-reduce ->
    :subgroup-reduce -> :baseline. The two rungs spell the same fold (the
    CUDA arm queries the width at runtime as warpSize where the warp row
    bakes 32), so the choice changes no reduction semantics -- it is a
    capability-evidence question, not an algorithmic one. Under
    ADR-0356 rule 6 and ADR-0357's capability precondition: the device
    descriptor must report, for the compute stage, the subgroup operations
    the fold needs (:subgroup-ops containing :basic and :shuffle-relative)
    and a subgroup size (:subgroup-size) that DIVIDES the workgroup -- which
    also refuses a subgroup larger than the workgroup, as rule 6 demands.
    The NVIDIA row carries the CUDA row's sm_70+ floor on top: whenever the
    descriptor reports a compute capability at all, one below 7.0 (or a
    malformed one) answers :baseline, fail closed; an absent capability is
    not a refusal, the reported subgroup facts are the capability evidence
    (ADR-0361).
    The reported width is taken as the device gives it (8/16/32 on Intel,
    4..32 on Mali, 32 on Apple's SIMD-groups, 32 or 64 as the shader
    chooses on RDNA, 32 -- the warp -- on NVIDIA
    Vulkan); the emission is parametric
    over it. The descriptor keys are the contract this table consumes and
    their producer DOES NOT EXIST YET: the wire-42 answers of the (today
    unimplemented) ADR-0371 GPU provider carry no subgroup facts (INFO is
    four fields, DEVCLASS one class word), so a probe or provider extension
    must report them -- the gap is named in ADR-0360 and the rows fail
    closed without them: a descriptor that reports none of them answers
    :baseline. The keys are the contract on EVERY route: the ADR-0362
    wire-42 SUBGROUP request is the Vulkan route's producer, and a
    Metal-native probe would fill the same keys directly from the device's
    SIMD-group facts (:basic / :shuffle-relative are this table's own
    capability vocabulary -- the two ops the fold needs -- not a Vulkan
    binding).

  - :simdgroup-reduce -- Apple (JUN-58, ADR-0373) Metal-native SIMD-group
    reduce, the reduce shape on the MSL route ONLY, and the MSL ladder's
    FIRST rung (rule 3 step 2): outside the carved-out measured class, an
    Apple MSL descriptor (:metal or :msl-v1) reporting the fold's own
    rule-6 fact (:simd-reduce in :subgroup-ops -- evidence the device's
    SIMD-group exposes simd_sum/simd_max/simd_min) and a :subgroup-size
    dividing W answers this variant; :basic/:shuffle-relative are the
    SHUFFLE fold's facts and are NOT required here (the cross-SIMD-group
    tree uses threadgroup barriers). A descriptor reporting the shuffle
    fold's facts but not :simd-reduce falls to :subgroup-reduce (the
    second rung, byte-for-byte the old answer); everything else answers
    :baseline (rule 1, total, no throw). The measured-cell carve-out
    outranks rung 1 everywhere but this variant's OWN flip cells: at
    W = 32, W = 64, W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on the
    measured
    class
    (\"m1 max\" AND
    reported subgroup size
    32) a descriptor reporting :simd-reduce answers THIS variant,
    MEASURED faster than :baseline (apple-simdgroup-flip-note) -- the
    only
    cells where this variant has hardware evidence. Those six are every
    width the row admits, so the class's W band is flipped end to end:
    the carve-out stands only where the flip's keys do not hold (e.g. a
    \"m1 max\" descriptor whose reported SIMD-group width is not 32) --
    the class's reduce-shape answers there are measured or decided and
    closed to design rows -- and the row is Apple's and the
    MSL route's alone. The fact's producer gap is ADR-0373's: the wire-42
    SUBGROUP vocabulary is ADR-0362's eight VkSubgroupFeatureFlagBits
    tokens and does NOT carry :simd-reduce (device-from-subgroup parses
    that vocabulary only), so a Metal-native probe or a provider extension
    must report the key -- the row fails closed without it. Every answer
    this row gives is UNMEASURED (:measured? false) except its own flip
    cells (W = 32, W = 64, W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on
    the measured class,
    MSL
    route,
    n = 1000003 -- the
    only cells hardware measured, and measured FASTER than :baseline).

  Everything else answers :baseline: other vendors (:unknown, :qualcomm
  and any name outside the five classified rows -- :amd has carried a
  designed row since ADR-0364), other targets, ewise kernels, sub-minimum
  workgroups, a malformed kernel (the COMPLETE KIR is validated first --
  shape checks alone would admit W=300 and worse), a device that confirms
  no capability, or no device at all.

  :measured? is the honesty lock: it flips only when hardware measurement
  (JUN-57 on NVIDIA, JUN-59 on Intel, JUN-60 on Mali, JUN-58 on Apple,
  JUN-84 on AMD) replaces the basis text of the row it stands on. A
  measurement can REFUTE a row's basis, not only validate it. ADR-0360's
  measurement-contract question (1) is DECIDED (2026-10-23, JUN-58): for
  the MSL arm a native Metal harness that dispatches the emitters' own
  bytes under the sweep protocol SATISFIES the contract -- wire-42 executes
  SPIR-V through Vulkan and can never run MSL, so the route clause was a
  deadlock for this arm rather than an honesty lock, and the properties it
  protects (the emitters' own bytes, the sweep protocol, verified
  read-back, a named device, a record under docs/evidence/) all hold. The
  flip is keyed per ANSWER CELL: the tested device class (the uB5G name
  fragment \"m1 max\" AND the reported subgroup size 32), the channel that
  measured the arm (:native-metal, the MSL route), and the measured
  workgroup widths (W = 64/128/256/512/1024 -- W = 32 measured too but
  DECIDED for the SHUFFLE fold, not verdict-chosen; that fold's W = 32
  answer keeps the lock, see
  apple-w32-note) -- plus this variant's OWN flip cells, the
  :simdgroup-reduce answers at W = 32, W = 64, W = 128, W = 256, W = 512
  and W = 1024
  where the
  fold's rule-6 fact is
  reported (2026-10-23/24's measurements of this variant, faster than
  :baseline at all six cells, apple-simdgroup-flip-note). Exactly those
  cells carry
  :measured? true and a :measured-cell record; every other answer keeps
  false. The 2026-10-19 wire-42 baseline-vs-variant runs (PR #1347/#1348)
  are NOT verdict-grade: their own record (docs/evidence/gpu/loadsweep-
  20261019/README.md) labels them LABELED BOOTSTRAP -- an unbalanced replay
  order biases the ratio beyond drift -- and publishes no verdict, so the
  GLSL arm keeps :measured? false at every width; those runs corroborate
  the sign and nothing more.

  ADR-0360's second decision (2026-10-23, JUN-58) NARROWS the Apple row
  to the measured device: a descriptor whose name parts (:device, :name,
  or the :vendor name key) carry the measured-device fragment \"m1 max\"
  (case-insensitively -- the uB5G idiom of
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets) answers :baseline for the reduce
  shape on BOTH Apple routes, annotated with the refutation in :basis.
  The narrowing acts exactly where the evidence is: every other Apple
  device -- including an unnamed {:vendor-id 4203} descriptor -- keeps
  the designed :subgroup-reduce row (UNMEASURED), and non-Apple vendors
  are untouched. A refuted device WITHOUT rule-6 facts is the ordinary
  fail-closed :baseline and carries no narrowing note (the note is only
  the row's own demotion). No report may
  cite an answer here as optimization evidence outside the flipped cells'
  own recorded scope (one device, one channel, the measured widths --
  and for :simdgroup-reduce its own flip cells only, W = 32, W = 64,
  W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and
  W = 1024,
  n = 1000003)."
  ([vendor kir] (select-variant vendor kir nil nil))
  ([vendor kir target] (select-variant vendor kir target nil))
  ([vendor kir target device]
   ;; ADR-0356 rule 3 (emission admission first): a selected variant is one
   ;; the route's emitter can emit -- all four emitters admit
   ;; :subgroup-reduce since 2026-10-23 (the CUDA arm landed that day), and
   ;; the CUDA emitter additionally carries :warp-shuffle-reduce. Admission
   ;; is NECESSARY, not sufficient: the ROWS below decide each route's
   ;; answer, and a route outside every row answers :baseline.
   (let [route (emitter-route target)
         dev (or device {})
         ;; rule: validate the COMPLETE KIR first (a malformed kernel never
         ;; selects a variant).
         valid-kir? (try (accelerator/validate! kir) true (catch :default _ false))
         ;; ADR-0356 rule 6 (device capability): the NVIDIA row's sm_70+
         ;; floor is a real requirement; a descriptor that does not confirm
         ;; compute capability 7.0+ answers :baseline (fail closed, no
         ;; guessing).
         cuda-cap-ok? (let [cc (:compute-capability dev)]
                        (and (number? cc) (>= cc 7.0)))
         ;; ADR-0361: on the Vulkan route NVIDIA joins the shared
         ;; subgroup-reduce row, and the CUDA row's sm_70+ floor binds that
         ;; row whenever the descriptor reports a compute capability at all
         ;; -- a reported capability below 7.0, or a malformed one, fails
         ;; closed to :baseline, while an absent capability is not a
         ;; refusal (the rule-6 subgroup facts are the capability evidence
         ;; there, exactly as for the Intel/ARM/Apple rows).
         nvidia-floor-ok? (let [cc (:compute-capability dev)]
                            (or (nil? cc) (and (number? cc) (>= cc 7.0))))
         ;; ADR-0356 rule 6 and ADR-0357's ARM gate: the fold needs the
         ;; basic and shuffle-relative subgroup ops in the compute stage
         ;; (what the emission's two GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_* extensions
         ;; name) and a subgroup size that divides the workgroup. Every
         ;; missing or malformed fact fails closed to :baseline -- a
         ;; descriptor that reports no capability is not a refusal, it is an
         ;; absence of evidence, and the portable variant is the answer.
         subgroup-cap-ok?
         (let [s (:subgroup-size dev)
               ops (:subgroup-ops dev)
               wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
           (and (int? s) (pos? s)
                (set? ops)
                (contains? ops :basic)
                (contains? ops :shuffle-relative)
                (int? wg) (pos? wg)
                (zero? (mod wg s))))
         ;; ADR-0373 rule 3 step 2, rule 6 for the :simdgroup-reduce fold:
         ;; the gate's vocabulary grows ONE fact, :simd-reduce -- evidence
         ;; that the device's SIMD-group exposes simd_sum/simd_max/simd_min
         ;; -- plus the reported :subgroup-size (= SIMD-group width)
         ;; dividing W. :basic and :shuffle-relative are the SHUFFLE fold's
         ;; facts and are NOT required here (the cross-SIMD-group tree uses
         ;; threadgroup barriers, not subgroup operations). Every missing
         ;; or malformed fact fails closed, exactly like every other row.
         simd-reduce-cap-ok?
         (let [s (:subgroup-size dev)
               ops (:subgroup-ops dev)
               wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
           (and (int? s) (pos? s)
                (set? ops)
                (contains? ops :simd-reduce)
                (int? wg) (pos? wg)
                (zero? (mod wg s))))
         ;; ADR-0360's second decision (2026-10-23, JUN-58): the Apple
         ;; row is NARROWED to the measured device. A descriptor naming
         ;; the measured device (its name parts carry the fragment
         ;; "m1 max", uB5G idiom) answers :baseline for the reduce shape
         ;; on every Apple route -- the Vulkan, MSL and (since the
         ;; 2026-10-24 Apple WGSL-route decision) WGSL routes -- the
         ;; row's own basis is refuted there
         ;; by hardware (the variant measured ~20-27% slower on the GLSL
         ;; arm and ~22% slower on the MSL arm; the WGSL arm on this
         ;; device is UNMEASURED and the conservative answer is the
         ;; measured one). Every other Apple device
         ;; keeps the designed row (UNMEASURED).
         apple-narrowed-device?
         (and (= :apple vendor)
              (device-name-carries? dev apple-measured-fragment))
         subgroup-row-holds?
         (and (contains? #{:intel :arm :apple :nvidia :amd} vendor)
              (or (= :glsl-v1 route)
                  ;; ADR-0363: the WGSL route (:webgpu /
                  ;; :wgsl-v1) carries the Intel and ARM rows;
                  ;; ADR-0364 adds :amd (its vendor-default
                  ;; target, the ADR-0363 rationale). The
                  ;; 2026-10-24 NVIDIA WGSL-route decision
                  ;; (docs/evidence/gpu/nvidia-wgsl-route-decision-
                  ;; 20261024.txt, JUN-57's own decision) adds
                  ;; :nvidia on the same rules 3 + 6 gate -- the
                  ;; sm_70+ floor below binds this rung exactly as
                  ;; it binds every NVIDIA rung, and no warp rung
                  ;; exists on this route (:warp-shuffle-reduce is
                  ;; CUDA emission only). The 2026-10-24 Apple
                  ;; WGSL-route decision
                  ;; (docs/evidence/gpu/apple-wgsl-route-decision-
                  ;; 20261024.txt, JUN-58's own decision) adds
                  ;; :apple on the same rules 3 + 6 gate -- the
                  ;; measured-device narrowing below rides along
                  ;; (the narrowed class answers :baseline on this
                  ;; route too; its WGSL arm is UNMEASURED and the
                  ;; refuted variant is not selected on the device
                  ;; that refuted it), and no simdgroup rung exists
                  ;; on this route (:simdgroup-reduce is MSL
                  ;; emission only).
                  (and (= :wgsl-v1 route)
                       (contains? #{:intel :arm :amd :nvidia :apple} vendor))
                  ;; ADR-0360's amendment (2026-10-23, JUN-58's
                  ;; own decision): the MSL route (:metal /
                  ;; :msl-v1) carries the Apple row, under the
                  ;; same rules 3 + 6 gate as the Vulkan route.
                  (and (= :msl-v1 route)
                       (= :apple vendor))
                  ;; ADR-0361's same-day amendment (JUN-57's own
                  ;; decision): the CUDA route (:cuda /
                  ;; :cuda-v1) carries the NVIDIA row as the
                  ;; SECOND rung of that route's ladder. The
                  ;; :warp-shuffle-reduce branch above is checked
                  ;; FIRST in this cond and keeps precedence
                  ;; whenever its gate holds (a CONFIRMED compute
                  ;; capability 7.0+ and W >= 32) -- every answer
                  ;; it gives is unchanged; this rung answers
                  ;; only where the warp row's gate does not
                  ;; reach (a descriptor reporting the rule-6
                  ;; subgroup facts and NO compute capability),
                  ;; under the same rules 3 + 6 gate, fail closed
                  ;; included.
                  (and (= :cuda-v1 route)
                       (= :nvidia vendor)))
              valid-kir?
              subgroup-cap-ok?
              ;; ADR-0361: NVIDIA's row carries the CUDA row's
              ;; sm_70+ floor; the other four vendors (AMD's
              ;; row, ADR-0364, included) have no
              ;; compute-capability contract and pass this gate
              ;; on the absent fact alone.
              (or (not= :nvidia vendor) nvidia-floor-ok?)
              (subgroup-reduce/applicable? kir))
         narrowed? (and subgroup-row-holds? apple-narrowed-device?)
         ;; ADR-0373 rule 3 step 2, the MSL ladder's FIRST rung: outside
         ;; the carved-out measured class, an Apple MSL descriptor that
         ;; reports the fold's rule-6 fact (:simd-reduce) and a reported
         ;; SIMD-group width dividing W answers :simdgroup-reduce for the
         ;; reduce shape. The row is Apple's alone (one vendor per
         ;; decision) and the MSL route's alone (rule 3: the emission is
         ;; admitted on :msl-v1 only). The carve-out is checked at the
         ;; cond below: the measured class reaches this rung only at this
         ;; variant's own flip cells (simd-flip?, below).
         simdgroup-row-holds?
         (and (= :apple vendor)
              (= :msl-v1 route)
              valid-kir?
              simd-reduce-cap-ok?
              (simdgroup/applicable? kir))
         ;; ADR-0373's flip contract applied to this variant's OWN
         ;; measured cells (2026-10-23/24, JUN-58): hardware measured THIS
         ;; variant faster than :baseline at W = 32, W = 64, W = 128,
         ;; W = 256,
         ;; W = 512 and
         ;; W = 1024
         ;; on the
         ;; measured class (apple-simdgroup-flip-note), so the carve-out
         ;; lifts exactly at those cells. The keys are the tested device class (the uB5G
         ;; name fragment AND the reported subgroup size 32), the
         ;; measured channel's route (the MSL route under either
         ;; spelling), this row's own rule-6 gate (the measurement ran
         ;; the intrinsic fold), and the workgroup widths the channel ran
         ;; for this variant. Everything else fails closed to the
         ;; carve-out. A shape enters a flip cell's :measured-cell record
         ;; only on the cell's OWN claim-protocol rule-2 verdict at that
         ;; shape -- REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER. A measured-but-scoped cell
         ;; (rule 2 REGIME-SCOPED) is REFUSED as a selection basis and
         ;; extends no record: the shape-2 W = 512 cell, 2026-10-24, the
         ;; first of its kind.
         simd-flip?
         (and simdgroup-row-holds?
              apple-narrowed-device?
              (= :msl-v1 route)
              (= apple-measured-subgroup-size (:subgroup-size dev))
              (contains? apple-simdgroup-measured-workgroups
                         (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)))
         variant (cond
                   (and (= :nvidia vendor)
                        (= :cuda-v1 route)
                        valid-kir?
                        cuda-cap-ok?
                        (= :reduce (:kernel/op kir))
                        (contains? #{:sum :max :min} (:kernel/operator kir))
                        (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
                          (and (int? wg) (>= wg min-warp-shuffle-workgroup))))
                   :warp-shuffle-reduce

                   ;; ADR-0373's measured-cell carve-out (measurement
                   ;; beats design) outranks rung 1 everywhere but this
                   ;; variant's OWN flip cells (simd-flip?): at W = 32,
                   ;; W = 64,
                   ;; W = 128,
                   ;; W = 256,
                   ;; W = 512 and W = 1024 on
                   ;; the measured class hardware measured THIS variant
                   ;; faster and the cells answer it, MEASURED -- every
                   ;; width the row admits, so the class's W band is
                   ;; flipped end to end and the narrowed M1 Max class
                   ;; keeps its reduce-shape answers only where the
                   ;; flip's keys do not hold. Rung 2 below
                   ;; is byte-for-byte the pre-step-2 answer.
                   (and simdgroup-row-holds?
                        (or (not apple-narrowed-device?) simd-flip?))
                   :simdgroup-reduce

                   (and subgroup-row-holds? (not narrowed?))
                   :subgroup-reduce

                   :else :baseline)
         ;; The wiring rows (the entry contracts' application): a
         ;; published cell verdict enters the selection surface here and
         ;; only here. PRECEDENCE (the seventh amendment's contract
         ;; point 1): cuda-entry-op is consulted FIRST -- a per-op record
         ;; answers its operator at its W OVER the pooled record (its
         ;; :operators key is #{op}); cuda-entry-cell (the pooled arm)
         ;; answers everywhere NO per-op record applies. Both arms key
         ;; the tested device class (xavier sm_72 -- the narrowed key:
         ;; both name fragments in one name part AND reported 7.2,
         ;; cuda-tested-class?), the CUDA route, and the reduce op; the pooled
         ;; arm adds an ENTERED (W, shape) group and the per-op arm an
         ;; ENTERED (op, W) group -- everything else is REFUSE and this
         ;; binding is nil (any other device class, any other route,
         ;; rung-2 answer cells -- the design ladder's answer stands
         ;; with no record; at W = 64 only the per-op table's three
         ;; entered groups (:sum/:max/:min) key anything -- the pooled
         ;; straddle refused W = 64 and the 15 non-materialized (op, W)
         ;; groups key nothing per-op).
         ;; :confirm freezes the answer text and attaches the record
         ;; ((:max, 64) and (:min, 64) are the two real confirms);
         ;; :flip answers the entry's winner ((:sum, 64) is the ONE
         ;; real differing winner -- the first real flip on this route).
         cuda-entry (or (cuda-entry-op dev route kir variant)
                        (cuda-entry-cell dev route kir variant))
         entry-flip? (= :flip (:outcome cuda-entry))
         ;; The carve-out's own note: it attaches only where rung 1's gate
         ;; held and the measured class refused the row -- the row's own
         ;; demotion, never a fail-closed :baseline, and never at this
         ;; variant's own flip cell (the measurement answered there, so
         ;; there is no demotion to annotate).
         carved? (and simdgroup-row-holds? apple-narrowed-device? (not simd-flip?))
         ;; ADR-0360 question (1)'s decision, Part 2 (2026-10-23, JUN-58):
         ;; the flip's record. A cell flips only when every key of the
         ;; tested device class holds -- the narrowed answer itself, the
         ;; measured device (the uB5G name fragment AND the reported
         ;; subgroup size 32 -- the contract keys on both), the measured
         ;; channel's route (the MSL route under either spelling; an MSL
         ;; measurement never flips a GLSL cell), and a workgroup width the
         ;; channel actually ran. The record names the class so no claim
         ;; extends past it; every other answer keeps :measured? false and
         ;; carries no record.
         ;; This variant's OWN flip records (the W = 512, W = 1024,
         ;; W = 128, W = 64 and W = 32
         ;; cells flipped in their own later slices): at each flip cell
         ;; the record names THIS variant's runs at that cell's own W and
         ;; its one measured shape -- the :subgroup-reduce runs say
         ;; nothing about this
         ;; variant (uB5G, reverse reading). The :evidence map is closed
         ;; under the flip contract's record kinds (each cell's own flip
         ;; runs and its sanctioned cross-day replication runs) and names
         ;; no other record kind: a same-day re-session is flip-note
         ;; prose only (the same-day re-session naming decision,
         ;; docs/evidence/gpu/same-day-resweep-naming-20261024.txt).
         simd-measured-cell
         (when simd-flip?
           {:device apple-measured-fragment
            :subgroup-size apple-measured-subgroup-size
            :channel apple-measured-channel
            :route :msl-v1
            :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
            :operators #{:sum :max :min}
            :shapes (get apple-simdgroup-measured-shapes
                         (:kernel/workgroup-size kir))
            :evidence (get apple-simdgroup-measured-evidence
                           (:kernel/workgroup-size kir))})
         measured-cell
         (or simd-measured-cell
             (:record cuda-entry)
             (when (and narrowed?
                        (= :msl-v1 route)
                        (= apple-measured-subgroup-size (:subgroup-size dev))
                        (contains? apple-measured-workgroups
                                   (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)))
               {:device apple-measured-fragment
                :subgroup-size apple-measured-subgroup-size
                :channel apple-measured-channel
                :route :msl-v1
                :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
                :operators #{:sum :max :min}
                :shapes apple-measured-shapes
                :evidence apple-measured-evidence}))]
    (cond-> {:variant (if entry-flip? (:variant cuda-entry) variant)
             :measured? (some? measured-cell)
             :basis (cond entry-flip?
                          ;; the flip arm (contract point 4): the cell
                          ;; answers the entry's winner, and its basis is
                          ;; the winner's design rationale plus the flip
                          ;; note naming the measurement that chose it
                          ;; (pooled entries carry cuda-flip-note, per-op
                          ;; entries cuda-op-flip-note -- the note rides
                          ;; the entry).
                          (str (get variant-bases (:variant cuda-entry)) "\n  "
                               (:flip-note cuda-entry))
                          simd-flip?
                          (str (get variant-bases :simdgroup-reduce) "\n  "
                               apple-simdgroup-flip-note)
                          narrowed?
                          (str (get variant-bases :baseline) "\n  " apple-narrow-note
                               (when (= 32 (:kernel/workgroup-size kir))
                                 (str "\n  " apple-w32-note))
                               (when carved?
                                 (str "\n  " apple-simdgroup-carveout-note)))
                          carved?
                          (str (get variant-bases :baseline) "\n  "
                               apple-simdgroup-carveout-note)
                          :else (get variant-bases variant))}
      measured-cell (assoc :measured-cell measured-cell)))))
